/* ===== Week 3 · story + theory ===== */
learn('t-bizcase', { ch: '3.1', title: 'Will it pay? The business case', line: 'Ms Mehta asks for a business case, explains Sahyadri\'s project portfolio, and sorts Saarthi\'s costs and benefits into the right boxes.' }, L => {
  H(L, STORY('3.1', 'Will it pay?', `
    ${NARR('Ms Mehta, Sahyadri\'s finance head, has fourteen project proposals on her desk and engineers for about ten of them.')}
    ${SAY('mehta', 'Riya, PCT\'s board said yes to Saarthi. My question is different: should <i>Sahyadri</i> take it? Every engineer on Saarthi is an engineer not on something else.')}
    ${SAY('riya', 'What do you need from me?')}
    ${SAY('mehta', 'A business case. What problem it solves, what it costs, what it earns, what could go wrong. And separate the risk that we fail to <i>build</i> it from the risk that, once built, it does not <i>pay</i>.')}
    ${SAY('dev', 'Some benefits are easy to count, like the cash leakage PCT stops. Some are not, like "passengers trust the buses more". Do we just ignore those?')}
    ${SAY('mehta', 'Managers love to ignore intangibles. Don\'t. Name them, even if you cannot price them.')}`));

  H(L, `<h2>1 · The business case</h2>
    <p>The feasibility study is also called the <b>business case</b>. Its main focus is to decide whether developing the software is <b>financially and technically feasible</b>. It provides the <b>justification for starting the project</b>: it must show that the benefits exceed the costs of development, implementation and operation (or production), and it must take account of business risks. Three types of feasibility study: <b>technical</b>, <b>financial</b> and <b>operational</b>.</p>
    <h3>The ten sections of a business case</h3>`);
  stepper(L, 'Building Saarthi\'s business case', SVG(640, 290, [['1 Introduction / background', 'problem to solve or opportunity'], ['2 The proposed project', 'brief outline of scope'], ['3 The market', 'likely demand (for a product)'], ['4 Organisational & operational\ninfrastructure', 'how the organisation must change'], ['5 The benefits', 'in financial terms, judged by the client'], ['6 Outline implementation plan', 'incl. disruption caused'], ['7 Costs', 'from the implementation plan'], ['8 The financial case', 'costs + benefits → value'], ['9 Risks', 'mainly BUSINESS risk'], ['10 Management plan', 'for smooth management']].map(([t, d], i) => `<g${S(i)}>${bx(10 + (i % 2) * 320, 8 + Math.floor(i / 2) * 56, 300, 46, '', { c: i === 8 ? 'red' : i === 7 ? 'grn' : 'blu' })}${tx(22 + (i % 2) * 320, 24 + Math.floor(i / 2) * 56, t.split('\n')[0], { anchor: 'start', c: 'sm' })}${tx(22 + (i % 2) * 320, 42 + Math.floor(i / 2) * 56, d, { anchor: 'start', c: 'xs mu' })}</g>`).join('')),
    ['<b>Introduction / background</b>: the problem to be solved or the opportunity to be exploited. Saarthi: passengers cannot tell when buses will arrive; cash leaks.', '<b>The proposed project</b>: a brief outline of the scope (app, depot portal, in-bus units).', '<b>The market</b>: for a new product (say a computer game) the likely demand must be assessed. For Saarthi: how many of Pune\'s daily riders have smartphones?', '<b>Organisational and operational infrastructure</b>: how the organisation must change. Important when a new information system is introduced: conductors will use handheld ticket machines; depots will plan from a portal.', '<b>The benefits</b>, normally in financial terms. In the end it is up to the client to assess them, since the client pays.', '<b>Outline implementation plan</b>: how the project will be implemented, considering the disruption it may cause (switching 2,000 buses without stopping service).', '<b>Costs</b>: the implementation plan supplies the information to establish them.', '<b>The financial case</b>: combines costs and benefits to establish the value of the project (chapter 3.2).', '<b>Risks</b>. Two types: <b>project risk</b> (threats to successful execution) and <b>business risk</b> (threats to the benefits of the delivered project). In a business case the main focus is <b>business risk</b>.', '<b>Management plan</b>: a detailed plan for smooth management. Ten sections in all.'], { ms: 2800 });
  choice(L, 'Which of these is a <b>business</b> risk for Saarthi rather than a project risk?', [
    ['The GPS vendor delivers the units three months late.', 'That threatens the execution of the project: a project risk.', false],
    ['After launch, most passengers keep paying cash and never install the app.', 'Right: the system is built fine, but its benefits do not materialise. Business case documents focus on this kind of risk.', true],
    ['Two senior developers resign mid-project.', 'A threat to execution: project risk.', false]], { tag: 'Quick check' });

  H(L, `<h2>2 · Project portfolio management (PPM)</h2>
    <p>Ms Mehta's desk is a portfolio. <b>PPM</b> provides an overview of all the projects an organisation is undertaking, prioritises the allocation of resources to them, and decides which new projects to accept and which existing ones to drop. Its concerns: evaluating proposals, assessing their risk, sharing resources between projects, taking account of dependencies, removing duplication, and making sure necessary developments have not been missed.</p>`);
  stepper(L, 'Three elements of PPM', SVG(640, 230, `
    ${bx(10, 20, 190, 70, '1 · Portfolio\ndefinition', { c: 'blu', at: S(0) })}${tx(105, 120, 'central record of all projects\n(all, or only ICT ones?)\nNPD vs renewal projects', { c: 'xs mu', at: S(0) })}
    ${ar(200, 55, 226, 55, { at: S(1) })}${bx(228, 20, 190, 70, '2 · Portfolio\nmanagement', { c: 'amb', at: S(1) })}${tx(323, 120, 'record and assess actual\ncosting and performance', { c: 'xs mu', at: S(1) })}
    ${ar(418, 55, 444, 55, { at: S(2) })}${bx(446, 20, 190, 70, '3 · Portfolio\noptimisation', { c: 'grn', at: S(2) })}${tx(541, 120, 'balance risky-but-valuable\nwith safe-but-modest projects', { c: 'xs mu', at: S(2) })}
    <g${S(3)}>${[['Saarthi', 'high value, high risk', 'red'], ['Bank KYC update', 'low risk, modest value', 'grn'], ['ERP for a dairy', 'medium', 'amb']].map(([t, d, c], i) => `${bx(40 + i * 200, 170, 170, 46, t + '\n' + d, { c, lh: 14 })}`).join('')}</g>`),
    ['<b>Portfolio definition</b>: create a central record of all projects in the organisation. Decide whether to include ALL projects or only, say, ICT projects. Note the difference between <b>new product development (NPD)</b> projects and <b>renewal</b> projects (e.g. process improvement).', '<b>Portfolio management</b>: the actual costing and performance of projects are recorded and assessed.', '<b>Portfolio optimisation</b>: use that information to balance the portfolio.', 'Example: Ms Mehta balances Saarthi (risky but potentially very valuable) with less risky but less valuable projects.']);
  H(L, `${VS([['PPM pros', '', ['Small ad-hoc tasks (quick fixes to systems) can still be done outside the portfolio']], ['PPM cons', '', ['Staff "allocated full-time" to a project may effectively be part-time: they still have routine work', 'If some projects are excluded, the official portfolio does not reflect actual activity']]])}
    <h2>3 · Evaluating an individual project</h2>
    ${COLS(DEF('Technical assessment', 'Can the required functionality be achieved with <b>current affordable technologies</b>? The cost of the chosen technology goes into the financial assessment.'), DEF('Financial assessment (cost-benefit analysis)', 'Identify all costs: <b>development</b> (staff salaries), <b>set-up</b> (hardware, recruitment, staff training), <b>operational</b> (running the system after installation). Identify the value of benefits. Check benefits exceed costs.'))}
    <h3>Cash-flow forecasting</h3>
    <p>Besides the totals, we need to know <b>when</b> expenditure and income will happen. Development costs (equipment, salaries) cannot wait until income arrives; once the product is released, its income gradually pays off the costs. Whether those early expenses can be funded from own resources or by borrowing depends on this forecast.</p>`);
  stepper(L, 'Typical product life-cycle cash flow', SVG(640, 250, `<line x1="50" y1="125" x2="620" y2="125" class="ln"/><line x1="50" y1="20" x2="50" y2="235" class="ln"/>${tx(28, 70, 'income', { c: 'xs grn' })}${tx(28, 190, 'expend-\niture', { c: 'xs red' })}
    ${[-30, -60, -80, -40, 20, 55, 75, 80, 70, 40].map((v, i) => `<rect x="${70 + i * 54}" y="${v < 0 ? 125 : 125 - v}" width="36" height="${Math.abs(v)}" rx="3" class="${v < 0 ? 'fill-r' : 'fill-g'}"${S(i < 4 ? 1 : 2)}/>`).join('')}
    <polyline points="${(() => { let c = 0; return [-30, -60, -80, -40, 20, 55, 75, 80, 70, 40].map((v, i) => { c += v; return `${88 + i * 54},${125 - c * 0.4}`; }).join(' '); })()}" class="ln thick b"${S(3)}/>
    ${tx(230, 222, 'development: money goes out', { c: 'xs red', at: S(1) })}${tx(470, 30, 'release: income pays the costs back', { c: 'xs grn', at: S(2) })}${tx(600, 50, 'cumulative', { c: 'xs brd', at: S(3) })}`),
    ['Years run left to right. Above the axis: income; below: expenditure.', 'During development the project only spends: equipment, salaries.', 'After release, income arrives and gradually pays off the costs, then tails off as the product ages.', 'The cumulative line shows how deep the hole gets (how much funding is needed) and when it climbs back above zero (the payback, chapter 3.2).']);

  H(L, `<h2>4 · Cost-benefit analysis: five steps</h2>
    ${STEPS(['<b>Identify</b> the costs and benefits of the project.', '<b>Categorise</b> them.', '<b>Select</b> a cost-benefit evaluation technique (net profit, payback, ROI, present value, NPV, IRR, break-even: chapter 3.2).', '<b>Interpret</b> the results: compare against a standard or an alternative investment; subjective, needs judgement and intuition; you may get a single value or a range (e.g. of NPV).', '<b>Take appropriate action</b>: select the most cost-effective, beneficial system and prepare the <b>feasibility study report</b> with findings and recommendations. The decision depends on the confidence in the estimates and the size of the investment.'])}
    <p><b>Identifying</b> is not always easy. Direct costs (the price of a computer) are obvious, and direct benefits often match them one-to-one (savings in the activity itself). Some are uncertain estimates (a reserve for bad debt). Hardest of all are <b>opportunity costs and benefits</b>: what you forgo by choosing one alternative over another (Ms Mehta's real question about Saarthi).</p>
    <h3>Three ways to categorise costs and benefits</h3>
    ${TBL(['Pair', 'Meaning', 'Lecture examples'], [['<b>Tangible</b> / intangible', 'Tangible: easily measured, an outlay of cash. Intangible: known to exist but the money value cannot be measured accurately (sometimes hard even to identify).', 'Tangible cost: hardware, software, training, salaries. Intangible cost: morale problems from a new system, lowered company image, an online banking system failing during banking hours (loses deposits, but by how much?). Tangible benefit: jobs in fewer hours, error-free reports. Intangible benefit: more satisfied customers, better corporate image.'], ['<b>Direct</b> / indirect', 'Direct: a money figure can be associated directly with the project. Indirect: results of operations not directly associated with the system: <b>overheads</b>, hard to apportion.', 'Direct cost: a $35 box of diskettes; development, set-up, operational costs. Direct benefit: new online system handles 25 % more transactions/day. Indirect cost: insurance, maintenance, protecting the computer centre from heat and fire, lighting, air conditioning. Indirect benefit: a by-product, e.g. a steel plant selling fertiliser, selling empty oil tins.'], ['<b>Fixed</b> / variable', 'Fixed: constant regardless of use; once incurred they do not recur. Variable: incurred regularly, roughly proportional to work volume, for as long as the system runs.', 'Fixed cost: straight-line depreciation of hardware, exempt employee salaries, insurance. Variable cost: computer forms in proportion to processing/report length. Fixed benefit: 20 % fewer staff thanks to the new computer (recurs monthly). Variable benefit: a safe-deposit tracking system saving 20 minutes per customer notice: savings vary with the number of notices.']])}
    <p>Both tangible and intangible items must be considered; management often deals irrationally with intangibles by ignoring them.</p>`);
  choice(L, 'Sahyadri\'s office air conditioning bill, part of which is "used" by the Saarthi team. What kind of cost is it for Saarthi?', [
    ['Direct cost', 'A direct cost can be tied to the project with a money figure. The AC is shared by all projects.', false],
    ['Indirect cost (overhead)', 'Right: lighting, air conditioning, insurance, protection of the computer centre are overheads; the share attributable to one project is hard to determine.', true],
    ['Intangible cost', 'It is easy to measure in rupees; it is just not attributable to Saarthi alone.', false]]);
  choice(L, 'PCT\'s ticket-printing paper: the more trips, the more rolls. Which category?', [
    ['Variable cost', 'Right: proportional to work volume and recurring while the system runs, like the lecture\'s computer forms.', true],
    ['Fixed cost', 'Fixed costs do not depend on how much the system is used (depreciation, insurance).', false],
    ['Indirect benefit', 'It is a cost, and directly linked to the operation.', false]], { tag: 'Quick check' });
  H(L, `<h3>Limitations of cost-benefit analysis</h3>
    ${COLS(DEF('Valuation problems', 'Intangible costs and benefits are difficult to quantify.'), DEF('Distortion problems', 'Intentional favouritism of an alternative for political reasons; incomplete or missing data.'), DEF('Completeness problems', 'The analysis may consider too many or too few costs.'))}`);
  H(L, RECAP('Business case = feasibility study: justify the project; benefits must exceed development, implementation and operation costs; consider business risk. Feasibility: technical, financial, operational.', 'Ten sections: introduction, proposed project, market, organisational & operational infrastructure, benefits, outline implementation plan, costs, financial case, risks, management plan.', 'Project risk (execution) vs business risk (benefits); business case focuses on business risk.', 'PPM: definition → management → optimisation; pros (ad-hoc work outside), cons (part-time "full-time" staff, incomplete portfolio).', 'CBA steps: identify, categorise, select technique, interpret, act. Categories: tangible/intangible, direct/indirect (overheads), fixed/variable. Opportunity costs. Limitations: valuation, distortion, completeness.'));
  H(L, NEXT('Ms Mehta lays four project proposals side by side, each with five years of cash flows. Which is the best? It depends on how you measure.', 't-cba', 'Chapter 3.2 · Four ways to say yes'));
});

learn('t-cba', { ch: '3.2', title: 'Four ways to say yes: net profit, payback, ROI, NPV, IRR', line: 'Four proposals, seven evaluation techniques, four different winners, and why money today is worth more than money tomorrow.' }, L => {
  H(L, STORY('3.2', 'Four ways to say yes', `
    ${NARR('Ms Mehta pins four cash-flow forecasts on the wall. They are the four projects of the lecture\'s example; she has given them nicknames.')}
    ${SAY('mehta', 'P1 is Saarthi\'s first phase: slow start, big payoff in year five. P2 is a huge ERP: a million dollars in, two hundred thousand a year out. P3 is a small, steady app. P4 is P3\'s bigger cousin with a final bonus. Which one?')}
    ${SAY('riya', 'P2 makes the most profit.')}
    ${SAY('mehta', 'On a million dollars invested. Try again, with each method I taught the interns, and watch the winner change.')}`));
  const P = { P1: [-100000, 10000, 10000, 10000, 20000, 100000], P2: [-1000000, 200000, 200000, 200000, 200000, 300000], P3: [-100000, 30000, 30000, 30000, 30000, 30000], P4: [-120000, 30000, 30000, 30000, 30000, 75000] };
  const npv = (c, r) => c.reduce((a, x, t) => a + x / (1 + r) ** t, 0);
  H(L, TBL(['Year', 'P1 ($)', 'P2 ($)', 'P3 ($)', 'P4 ($)'], [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5].map(y => [y, ...['P1', 'P2', 'P3', 'P4'].map(k => P[k][y].toLocaleString('en-US'))]).concat([['<b>Net profit</b>', ...['P1', 'P2', 'P3', 'P4'].map(k => `<b>${P[k].reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0).toLocaleString('en-US')}</b>`)]])));
  H(L, `<p>"Year 0" holds all the costs before the system is in operation; a cash flow is income minus outgoings. The lecture lists <b>seven</b> techniques: net profit, payback period, return on investment, present value, net present value, internal rate of return and break-even analysis.</p>
    <h2>1 · Net profit</h2>
    <p><b>Net profit</b> = total income − total costs over the life of the project (the sum of all cash flows). P1: −100,000 + 10,000 + 10,000 + 10,000 + 20,000 + 100,000 = <b>$50,000</b>. P2 shows the largest net profit ($100,000), but at the expense of a huge investment. <b>Con</b>: it ignores the <b>timing</b> of the cash flows.</p>
    <h2>2 · Payback period</h2>
    <p>The time taken to <b>break even</b>, i.e. to pay back the initial investment: when the accumulated cash flow turns non-negative. The shortest payback is preferred, because the owner wants to minimise the time the project is "in debt".</p>`);
  const acc = c => { let s = 0; return c.map(x => (s += x)); }; const usd = v => Math.round(v).toLocaleString('en-US');
  stepper(L, 'Accumulated cash flow: when does each project pay back?', SVG(640, 260, `<line x1="60" y1="140" x2="620" y2="140" class="ln"/><line x1="60" y1="20" x2="60" y2="255" class="ln"/>${[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5].map(y => tx(80 + y * 104, 156, 'Y' + y, { c: 'xs mu' })).join('')}${tx(36, 140, '0', { c: 'xs mu' })}
    ${['P1', 'P3', 'P4'].map((k, j) => `<polyline points="${acc(P[k]).map((v, y) => `${80 + y * 104},${140 - v * 0.0009}`).join(' ')}" class="ln thick ${['b', 'g', 'a'][j]}"${S(j + 1)}/>${tx(600 + j * 14, 140 - acc(P[k])[5] * 0.0009 - 10 - j * 4, k, { c: 'sm ' + ['brd', 'grn', 'hot'][j], at: S(j + 1) })}`).join('')}
    <g${S(4)}><circle cx="${80 + 4 * 104}" cy="${140 - 20000 * 0.0009}" r="7" class="fill-g pulse"/>${tx(80 + 4 * 104, 100, 'P3 above zero in year 4', { c: 'xs grn' })}</g>`),
    ['Accumulated cash flow, year by year; the zero line is break-even.', '<b>P1</b>: −100k → −90k → −80k → −70k → −50k → +50k. It pays back only in <b>year 5</b>.', '<b>P3</b>: −100k → −70k → −40k → −10k → +20k: pays back in <b>year 4</b>.', '<b>P4</b>: −120k → −90k → −60k → −30k → 0 → +75k: pays back <b>at the end of year 4</b> (P2 needs year 5).', 'So by payback, <b>P3</b> is the most beneficial. Pros: simple, not very sensitive to small forecasting errors. Cons: ignores overall profitability and any income after break-even: P2 and P4 are more profitable overall than P3, and payback ignores that.']);
  H(L, `<h2>3 · Return on investment (ROI)</h2>
    ${F('ROI = (average annual profit ÷ total investment) × 100', 'P1: (50,000 ÷ 5) ÷ 100,000 × 100 = 10 %')}
    <p>It compares net profitability with the investment and is a simple measure of return on capital. ROIs: P1 <b>10 %</b>, P2 <b>2 %</b> (100,000 ÷ 5 = 20,000, ÷ 1,000,000), P3 <b>10 %</b>, P4 <b>12.5 %</b> (75,000 ÷ 5 = 15,000, ÷ 120,000). So by ROI, <b>P4</b> wins. <b>Cons</b>: like net profit, it ignores the timing of the cash flows, so it bears no relationship to bank interest rates and is potentially very misleading.</p>
    <h2>4 · Time value of money: present value</h2>
    <p>Would you rather have Rs. 1,000 today or Rs. 1,000 in four years? Today: you could invest it. The <b>time value of money</b> (interest, inflation and other factors) means future money must be <b>discounted</b>. The discount rate is the return the money could earn if invested elsewhere.</p>
    ${F('Future value F = P (1 + r)<sup>t</sup>   →   present value P = F / (1 + r)<sup>t</sup>   ·   discount factor DF = 1 / (1 + r)<sup>t</sup>', 'r = 10 %: DF₁ = 0.9091, DF₂ = 0.8264')}
    <p>The lecture's example: $3,000 invested today for $1,500 of benefit per year over four years. The $1,500 at the end of year 4 is worth today P = 1,500/(1.10)<sup>4</sup> = 1,500/1.4641 ≈ <b>$1,024.52</b> (the slide rounds 1.4641 to 1.46 and gets $1,027.39): invest that much today at 10 % and you have $1,500 in four years.</p>`);
  stepper(L, 'Discounting: how much is $1,000 of future money worth today at 10 %?', SVG(640, 220, `<line x1="60" y1="190" x2="620" y2="190" class="ln"/>${[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5].map(t => { const v = 1000 / 1.1 ** t; return `<g${S(t)}><rect x="${80 + t * 90}" y="${190 - v * 0.16}" width="50" height="${v * 0.16}" rx="3" class="${t ? 'fill-v' : 'fill-g'}"/>${tx(105 + t * 90, 180 - v * 0.16, '$' + Math.round(v), { c: 'xs' })}${tx(105 + t * 90, 206, 'year ' + t, { c: 'xs mu' })}</g>`; }).join('')}`),
    ['$1,000 received today is worth $1,000.', 'Received after 1 year: 1,000 × 0.9091 = $909 today.', 'After 2 years: × 0.8264 = $826.', 'After 3 years: × 0.7513 = $751.', 'After 4 years: × 0.6830 = $683.', 'After 5 years: × 0.6209 = $621. The further away, the less it is worth now. That is why P1\'s big year-5 payoff is weaker than it looks.']);
  H(L, `<h2>5 · Net present value (NPV)</h2>
    <p>NPV takes into account both the profitability and the <b>timing</b> of the cash flows: <b>discount each cash flow (negative and positive) and add them up</b>. Year 0 is not discounted (DF = 1).</p>`);
  const DF = [1, 0.9091, 0.8264, 0.7513, 0.683, 0.6209];
  stepper(L, 'NPV of P1 at 10 %, row by row', SVG(640, 270, `${['Year', 'Cash flow', '× DF', '= discounted'].map((t, i) => tx(80 + i * 150, 18, t, { c: 'sm mu' })).join('')}
    ${P.P1.map((c, y) => `<g${S(y)}>${tx(80, 46 + y * 34, y)}${tx(230, 46 + y * 34, usd(c), { c: c < 0 ? 'red' : '' })}${tx(380, 46 + y * 34, DF[y].toFixed(4), { c: 'mono' })}${tx(530, 46 + y * 34, usd(c * DF[y]), { c: c < 0 ? 'red' : 'grn' })}</g>`).join('')}
    <g${S(6)}><line x1="440" y1="244" x2="620" y2="244" class="ln"/>${tx(380, 260, 'NPV', { c: 'lg' })}${tx(530, 260, '≈ +618', { c: 'lg grn' })}</g>`),
    ['Year 0: −100,000 × 1.0000 = −100,000 (today, no discount).', 'Year 1: 10,000 × 0.9091 = 9,091.', 'Year 2: 10,000 × 0.8264 = 8,264.', 'Year 3: 10,000 × 0.7513 = 7,513.', 'Year 4: 20,000 × 0.6830 = 13,660.', 'Year 5: 100,000 × 0.6209 = 62,090. The big payoff shrinks by more than a third.', 'Sum: <b>NPV ≈ $618</b>. P1 barely beats a 10 % bank deposit. Do the same for the others: P2 ≈ <b>−179,770</b>, P3 ≈ <b>13,721</b>, P4 ≈ <b>21,662</b> → by NPV, <b>P4</b> is best and P2 should be rejected.'], { ms: 2600 });
  H(L, `<p><b>NPV's difficulty</b>: selecting an appropriate discount rate; and the result may not be directly comparable with earnings from other investments or the cost of borrowing. The standard table of discount factors (rates 5-15 %, years 1-25) is given in exams; DF falls with both the rate and the number of years.</p>
    <h2>6 · Internal rate of return (IRR)</h2>
    <p><b>IRR</b> is the <b>discount rate that makes NPV = 0</b>. It gives profitability as a <b>percentage</b> directly comparable with interest rates: a project with IRR 10 % is worthwhile if capital can be borrowed for less than 10 %, or cannot be invested elsewhere for more than 10 %. Spreadsheets compute it (Excel's IRR() takes the cash flows and an initial guess).</p>`);
  const curve = k => { const pts = []; for (let r = 0; r <= 0.3001; r += 0.01) pts.push(`${(70 + r * 1800).toFixed(1)},${(130 - npv(P[k], r) / 1000).toFixed(1)}`); return pts.join(' '); };
  const irr = c => { let lo = -0.5, hi = 1; for (let i = 0; i < 80; i++) { const m = (lo + hi) / 2; npv(c, m) > 0 ? lo = m : hi = m; } return lo; };
  stepper(L, 'IRR = where the NPV curve crosses zero', SVG(640, 260, `<line x1="70" y1="130" x2="630" y2="130" class="ln"/><line x1="70" y1="20" x2="70" y2="250" class="ln"/>${[0, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30].map(r => tx(70 + r * 18, 254, r + '%', { c: 'xs mu' })).join('')}${tx(40, 70, 'NPV\n($k)', { c: 'xs mu' })}
    ${['P1', 'P3', 'P4'].map((k, j) => `<polyline points="${curve(k)}" class="ln thick ${['b', 'g', 'a'][j]}"${S(j + 1)}/>`).join('')}
    ${['P1', 'P3', 'P4'].map((k, j) => { const r = irr(P[k]); return `<g${S(j + 1)}><circle cx="${70 + r * 1800}" cy="130" r="6" class="${['fill-b', 'fill-g', 'fill-a'][j]}"/>${tx(70 + r * 1800 + (j === 2 ? 34 : -30), 104 - j * 18, `${k} ${(r * 100).toFixed(1)}%`, { c: 'xs ' + ['brd', 'grn', 'hot'][j] })}</g>`; }).join('')}
    <line x1="${70 + 0.1 * 1800}" y1="20" x2="${70 + 0.1 * 1800}" y2="250" class="ln dash"${S(4)}/>${tx(70 + 0.1 * 1800, 232, 'cost of capital 10 %', { c: 'xs', at: S(4) })}`),
    ['NPV changes with the discount rate: the higher the rate, the less future income is worth.', 'P1\'s NPV curve crosses zero at about <b>10.2 %</b>: its IRR.', 'P3 crosses at about <b>15.2 %</b>.', 'P4 crosses at about <b>15.9 %</b>.', 'Any project whose IRR is above the cost of capital (here 10 %) is worth doing. <b>Cons of IRR</b>: it does not show the <b>absolute size</b> of the return (NPV $100,000 at IRR 15 % can beat NPV $10,000 at IRR 18 %), and sometimes more than one rate gives NPV = 0; then take the lowest.']);
  H(L, `<h2>7 · Break-even analysis</h2>
    <p>Break-even is the point where the cost of the <b>candidate</b> (proposed) system equals that of the <b>current</b> one. Unlike payback (costs vs benefits of the candidate), break-even compares the <b>costs of the current and candidate systems</b>. Initially the candidate costs more: the <b>investment period</b>. At break-even the costs are equal; beyond it the candidate gives greater benefit: the <b>return period</b>.</p>`);
  H(L, SVG(640, 250, `<line x1="60" y1="220" x2="620" y2="220" class="ln"/><line x1="60" y1="220" x2="60" y2="20" class="ln"/>${tx(340, 242, 'transactions processed →', { c: 'xs mu' })}${tx(34, 120, 'processing\ncost', { c: 'xs mu' })}
    <path d="M60 180 L 600 40" class="ln thick r draw" style="--len:600"/>${tx(460, 26, 'current (manual): low fixed,\nhigh variable cost', { c: 'xs red' })}
    <path d="M60 120 L 600 90" class="ln thick g draw" style="--len:600"/>${tx(560, 110, 'candidate: high fixed,\nlow variable cost', { c: 'xs grn' })}
    <polygon points="60,180 60,120 310,106" class="fill-a op3"/><polygon points="310,106 600,90 600,40" class="fill-g op3"/>
    <circle cx="310" cy="106" r="7" class="fill-p pulse"/>${tx(310, 126, "break-even (B')", { c: 'sm pur' })}${tx(310, 190, 'e.g. 65,000 transactions', { c: 'xs pur' })}
    ${tx(150, 160, 'investment\nperiod', { c: 'xs hot' })}${tx(540, 82, 'return period', { c: 'xs grn' })}`));
  H(L, `<p>In the lecture's chart (Awad, 2009) the lines cross at B' = <b>65,000 transactions</b>: below that volume manual processing is cheaper; above it the candidate system is.</p>
    ${TBL(['Technique', 'Winner among P1-P4', 'Main weakness'], [['Net profit', 'P2', 'ignores timing and investment size'], ['Payback', 'P3', 'ignores income after break-even'], ['ROI', 'P4 (12.5 %)', 'ignores timing'], ['NPV at 10 %', 'P4 (≈ 21,662)', 'choice of discount rate'], ['IRR', 'P4 (≈ 15.9 %)', 'ignores absolute size; multiple roots']])}`);
  choice(L, 'A colleague says: "P2 has the biggest net profit, so we take P2." What is wrong?', [
    ['Nothing: profit is what matters.', 'Net profit ignores the $1,000,000 tied up and the timing; P2\'s ROI is 2 % and its NPV at 10 % is about −$180,000.', false],
    ['Net profit ignores both the size of the investment and the timing of cash flows; discounted, P2 destroys value.', 'Right. NPV (and IRR) account for timing; P4 is the best at 10 %.', true],
    ['P2 has the longest payback.', 'P2 pays back in year 5, like P1; payback is not the main issue.', false]]);
  quickcheck(L, [['ROI of a project: investment 120,000; net profit over 5 years 75,000?', '(75,000 ÷ 5) ÷ 120,000 × 100 = <b>12.5 %</b>.'], ['Discount factor for year 2 at 10 %?', '1/(1.10)² = 0.8264.'], ['IRR in one sentence?', 'The discount rate at which NPV = 0; compare it with the cost of capital.'], ['What does break-even compare that payback does not?', 'Costs of the current system vs costs of the candidate system (payback compares the candidate\'s costs with its benefits).']]);
  H(L, RECAP('Net profit = Σ cash flows (ignores timing). Payback = year accumulated cash flow ≥ 0 (ignores later income).', 'ROI = average annual profit ÷ total investment × 100 (ignores timing; misleading).', 'PV = F/(1+r)<sup>t</sup>; DF = 1/(1+r)<sup>t</sup>; NPV = Σ discounted cash flows (year 0 undiscounted); difficulty: choosing r.', 'IRR = rate giving NPV 0, comparable with interest rates; hides absolute size, may have several roots (take the lowest).', 'Break-even: current vs candidate system costs; investment period → B\' → return period.', 'Lecture P1-P4: P1 NPV 618; P2 −179,770; P3 13,721; P4 21,662 (best).'));
  H(L, NEXT('All these numbers assume the forecasts come true. They won\'t, exactly. How do you put uncertainty into the decision?', 't-riskeval', 'Chapter 3.3 · When the future is uncertain'));
});

learn('t-riskeval', { ch: '3.3', title: 'When the future is uncertain: evaluating risk', line: 'Risk matrices, risk premiums, expected values, sensitivity analysis and a decision tree that says "extend, don\'t replace".' }, L => {
  H(L, STORY('3.3', 'When the future is uncertain', `
    ${SAY('mehta', 'Your NPV assumes 60,000 app users a day. What if it is 20,000? What if a Bengaluru startup gives PCT a free app next year?')}
    ${SAY('riya', 'Then Saarthi\'s numbers collapse.')}
    ${SAY('mehta', 'So don\'t give me one number. Give me the risks, how likely each is, how much they would hurt, and which assumption matters most.')}
    ${SAY('kul', 'And remember: a project can fail not because it was badly managed, but because it should never have been started.')}`));
  H(L, `<h2>1 · Two kinds of risk, five methods</h2>
    <p>Every project has some risk. <b>Project risk</b> threatens successful execution; <b>business risk</b> threatens the benefits of the delivered project. When evaluating a project (the business case) the main focus is <b>business risk</b>. The lecture gives five methods, from simplest to richest:</p>
    ${STEPS(['Risk identification and ranking', 'Risk and NPV (risk premium)', 'Cost-benefit analysis with probabilities (expected value)', 'Risk profile analysis (sensitivity analysis)', 'Decision trees'])}
    <h2>2 · Risk identification and ranking</h2>
    <p>Identify the risks and quantify their effects. Build a <b>project risk matrix</b> from a checklist of possible risks, classifying each by <b>importance</b> and <b>likelihood</b>, assessed <b>separately</b>: a serious but very unlikely risk may matter less than a less serious one that is almost certain. Project A may look better than B on returns yet be riskier; a matrix for each shows it. The lecture's matrix for an online shop:</p>`);
  const RM = [['Client rejects proposed look and feel of site', 'H', '—'], ['Competitors undercut prices', 'H', 'M'], ['Warehouse unable to deal with increased demand', 'M', 'L'], ['Online payment has security problems', 'M', 'M'], ['Maintenance costs higher than estimated', 'L', 'L'], ['Response times deter purchasers', 'M', 'M']];
  const lv = { H: 2, M: 1, L: 0, '—': -1 };
  H(L, SVG(640, 300, `${tx(320, 14, 'likelihood →', { c: 'xs mu' })}${['L', 'M', 'H'].map((t, i) => tx(230 + i * 140, 32, t, { c: 'sm mu' })).join('')}${tx(30, 160, 'impor-\ntance', { c: 'xs mu' })}${['H', 'M', 'L'].map((t, i) => tx(70, 80 + i * 80, t, { c: 'sm mu' })).join('')}
    ${[0, 1, 2].map(r => [0, 1, 2].map(c => `<rect x="${160 + c * 140}" y="${44 + r * 80}" width="136" height="76" rx="6" class="${(2 - r) + c >= 3 ? 'fill-r op3' : (2 - r) + c >= 2 ? 'fill-a op3' : 'fill-g op3'}"/>`).join('')).join('')}
    ${(() => { const cnt = {}; return RM.filter(r => r[2] !== '—').map(([t, i, l]) => { const key = i + l, k = cnt[key] = (cnt[key] || 0) + 1; return tx(228 + lv[l] * 140, 52 + (2 - lv[i]) * 80 + k * 22, t.length > 26 ? t.slice(0, 24) + '…' : t, { c: 'xs' }); }).join(''); })()}
    ${tx(320, 290, '"Client rejects look and feel": importance H, likelihood not given on the slide', { c: 'xs mu' })}`, 'wide'));
  H(L, `<h2>3 · Risk and NPV: the risk premium</h2>
    <p>For a relatively risky project it is common to use a <b>higher discount rate</b> when calculating NPV. This <b>risk premium</b> might be an additional <b>2 %</b> for a reasonably safe project or <b>5 %</b> for a fairly risky one. Projects can be scored as high, medium or low risk with a premium per category. Even if arbitrary, premiums give a <b>consistent</b> way of taking risk into account.</p>`);
  const P1 = [-100000, 10000, 10000, 10000, 20000, 100000], P3 = [-100000, 30000, 30000, 30000, 30000, 30000];
  const npv = (c, r) => c.reduce((a, x, t) => a + x / (1 + r) ** t, 0);
  stepper(L, 'Same cash flows, rising risk premium', SVG(640, 220, `<line x1="60" y1="120" x2="620" y2="120" class="ln"/>${[[10, 'base 10 %'], [12, '+2 % (safe)'], [15, '+5 % (fairly risky)']].map(([r, t], i) => { const a = npv(P1, r / 100) / 300, b2 = npv(P3, r / 100) / 300; return `<g${S(i)}><rect x="${90 + i * 180}" y="${a > 0 ? 120 - a : 120}" width="50" height="${Math.abs(a)}" rx="3" class="fill-v"/><rect x="${145 + i * 180}" y="${b2 > 0 ? 120 - b2 : 120}" width="50" height="${Math.abs(b2)}" rx="3" class="fill-g"/>${tx(115 + i * 180, a > 0 ? 112 - a : 136 - a, Math.round(npv(P1, r / 100)).toLocaleString('en-US'), { c: 'xs brd' })}${tx(170 + i * 180, b2 > 0 ? 112 - b2 : 136 - b2, Math.round(npv(P3, r / 100)).toLocaleString('en-US'), { c: 'xs grn' })}${tx(142 + i * 180, 206, t, { c: 'sm' })}</g>`; }).join('')}${tx(540, 30, '■ P1  ', { c: 'sm brd' })}${tx(590, 30, '■ P3', { c: 'sm grn' })}`),
    ['At the normal 10 % rate, P1\'s NPV is about +621 and P3\'s about +13,724.', 'Treat both as "reasonably safe" (+2 % → 12 %): P1 turns negative; P3 stays positive.', 'Treat them as "fairly risky" (+5 % → 15 %): P1 is clearly rejected; P3 is just above zero. Back-loaded cash flows (P1) are punished most by a premium, because the far-future payoffs are discounted hardest.']);
  H(L, `<h2>4 · Cost-benefit analysis with probabilities</h2>
    <p>A more sophisticated approach: consider each possible outcome, estimate its <b>probability</b> and its value, and sum value × probability (the <b>expected value</b>). Instead of a single cash-flow forecast you have a set of forecasts, each with a probability. The lecture's example: a company plans a <b>payroll application</b>.</p>
    ${TBL(['Sales', 'Annual income (i)', 'Probability (p)', 'i × p'], [['High (targets efficiently, no competitor)', '$800,000', '0.1', '$80,000'], ['Medium (launches first, gains market lead)', '$650,000', '0.6', '$390,000'], ['Low (competitor launches first)', '$100,000', '0.3', '$30,000'], ['<b>Expected income</b>', '', '', '<b>$500,000 / year</b>']])}
    <p>Development cost $750,000; sales constant for at least 4 years; annual costs $200,000. Expected net income over 4 years = 4 × 500,000 − 4 × 200,000 = <b>$1,200,000</b>: a good return on $750,000. <b>But</b> there is a 30 % chance of low sales, and then income (100,000) does not even cover annual costs (200,000): the company loses money. The lecture's advice: <b>not advisable</b> to take up this risky project.</p>
    <p><b>Limitations</b>: assigning probabilities is a challenge, and the method does not take full account of worst-case scenarios (the expected value hides the 30 % disaster).</p>
    <h2>5 · Risk profile analysis (sensitivity analysis)</h2>
    <p>To overcome those problems, build <b>risk profiles</b> using <b>sensitivity analysis</b>: vary each factor in turn and see how much the outcome changes. This identifies the factors most important to success. Then decide whether you can exercise better control over them or mitigate their effects; if neither, live with the risk or abandon the project. Riya's sensitivity analysis of Saarthi's NPV (each factor ±20 %):</p>`);
  stepper(L, 'Which assumption matters most? (illustrative)', SVG(640, 250, `<line x1="320" y1="20" x2="320" y2="230" class="ln"/>${tx(320, 244, 'base NPV', { c: 'xs mu' })}
    ${[['daily app users', 150], ['ticket price', 120], ['development cost', 70], ['maintenance cost', 45], ['server hosting cost', 18]].map(([t, w], i) => `<g${S(i + 1)}><rect x="${320 - w}" y="${30 + i * 40}" width="${w}" height="26" rx="3" class="fill-r op5"/><rect x="320" y="${30 + i * 40}" width="${w}" height="26" rx="3" class="fill-g op5"/>${tx(320 - w - 8, 43 + i * 40, t, { anchor: 'end', c: 'xs' })}</g>`).join('')}
    ${tx(160, 14, 'NPV if the factor goes the bad way', { c: 'xs red' })}${tx(480, 14, 'NPV if it goes the good way', { c: 'xs grn' })}`),
    ['Start from the base-case NPV and vary one factor at a time by ±20 %.', 'The number of daily app users swings NPV the most: the critical factor.', 'Ticket price is next (PCT sets fares, so Sahyadri cannot control it).', 'Development cost matters less (and is under Riya\'s control).', 'Maintenance cost: smaller.', 'Hosting cost hardly matters. Decision: Saarthi\'s risk is mainly adoption. Riya proposes a mitigation: a 10 % discount on app tickets for the first year, to drive users.']);
  H(L, `<h2>6 · Decision trees</h2>
    <p>The previous approaches treat the manager as a passive bystander who can only accept or reject. Sometimes you must choose a <b>course of action</b> that limits or affects future options; a <b>decision tree</b> evaluates how each decision affects future profitability. The lecture's example: a company must decide whether to <b>extend</b> its sales order processing system or <b>replace</b> it now. The right choice depends on whether its market expands (probability 0.2) or not (0.8).</p>`);
  stepper(L, 'Extend or replace? Evaluating the tree', SVG(660, 280, `
    <rect x="16" y="122" width="36" height="36" class="bx b"/>${tx(34, 140, 'D', { c: 'wh' })}
    ${pth('M52 140 L 200 70', { m: false })}${pth('M52 140 L 200 210', { m: false })}${tx(110, 86, 'extend', { c: 'sm' })}${tx(110, 196, 'replace', { c: 'sm' })}
    <circle cx="214" cy="70" r="14" class="bx amb"/><circle cx="214" cy="210" r="14" class="bx amb"/>
    ${pth('M228 70 L 400 30', { m: false })}${pth('M228 70 L 400 110', { m: false })}${pth('M228 210 L 400 170', { m: false })}${pth('M228 210 L 400 250', { m: false })}
    ${tx(300, 36, 'expands 0.2', { c: 'xs' })}${tx(300, 104, 'no 0.8', { c: 'xs' })}${tx(300, 176, 'expands 0.2', { c: 'xs' })}${tx(300, 244, 'no 0.8', { c: 'xs' })}
    ${tx(470, 30, '−$100,000', { c: 'sm red', at: S(1) })}${tx(470, 110, '+$75,000', { c: 'sm grn', at: S(1) })}${tx(470, 170, '+$250,000', { c: 'sm grn', at: S(2) })}${tx(470, 250, '−$50,000', { c: 'sm red', at: S(2) })}
    ${tx(590, 70, 'EV = +40,000', { c: 'sm grn', at: S(3) })}${tx(590, 210, 'EV = +10,000', { c: 'sm', at: S(4) })}
    <g${S(5)}>${pth('M52 140 L 200 70', { c: 'g thick', m: false })}${tx(590, 92, '✔ choose extend', { c: 'sm grn' })}</g>`, 'wide'),
    ['Decision point D (square): extend the existing system, or replace it now. Each leads to a chance node (circle): the market expands (0.2) or not (0.8).', '<b>Extend</b>: if the market does not expand, NPV = +$75,000; if it expands, the old system cannot cope and lost revenue makes it −$100,000.', '<b>Replace</b>: if the market expands, NPV = +$250,000 (handles increased sales, better MIS); if not, the benefits are severely reduced: −$50,000 (the slide\'s text says −500,000, but its calculation, like the textbook, uses −50,000).', 'Expected value of extending = 0.8 × 75,000 − 0.2 × 100,000 = <b>+$40,000</b>.', 'Expected value of replacing = 0.2 × 250,000 − 0.8 × 50,000 = <b>+$10,000</b>.', 'The company should <b>extend</b> the existing system: the path with the higher expected value.']);
  choice(L, 'If the probability of the market expanding rose to 0.5, what would the tree say?', [
    ['Still extend.', 'Extend: 0.5 × 75,000 − 0.5 × 100,000 = −12,500. Replace: 0.5 × 250,000 − 0.5 × 50,000 = +100,000.', false],
    ['Replace: its expected value becomes +100,000 vs −12,500 for extending.', 'Right. A decision tree shows exactly how the best decision depends on the probabilities (try the lab in the exam toolkit below).', true]]);
  H(L, `${IDEA('Three lessons from the lecture summary', '<ul><li>A project may fail not through poor management but because it should never have been started.</li><li>A project may make a profit, but something else might make even more.</li><li>Projects with the highest potential returns are often the most risky.</li></ul>')}`);
  quickcheck(L, [['What is a risk premium and typical values?', 'An addition to the discount rate for risky projects: about 2 % for reasonably safe, 5 % for fairly risky ones.'], ['Expected annual income: 800k (0.1), 650k (0.6), 100k (0.3)?', '80k + 390k + 30k = $500,000.'], ['What does sensitivity analysis tell you?', 'Which factors the outcome is most sensitive to, so you can control or mitigate them (or accept or abandon).']]);
  H(L, RECAP('Project risk (execution) vs business risk (benefits); evaluation focuses on business risk.', 'Risk matrix: importance and likelihood assessed separately.', 'Risk premium: +2 % safe, +5 % fairly risky on the discount rate.', 'CBA with probabilities: expected value = Σ value × probability; payroll example: expected $500k/yr but 30 % chance of loss → not advisable. Limits: probabilities hard; worst case hidden.', 'Risk profile = sensitivity analysis → find the critical factors.', 'Decision tree: extend EV 40,000 vs replace EV 10,000 → extend.'));
  H(L, NEXT('Saarthi is approved. Then Mr Bhosale mentions that it is "one part of the city\'s smart mobility programme". Riya realises her project is a piece of something bigger.', 't-programme', 'Chapter 3.4 · Part of something bigger'));
});

learn('t-programme', { ch: '3.4', title: 'Part of something bigger: programmes and benefits', line: 'Saarthi is one project in PCT\'s Smart Mobility programme; programme managers think differently, and benefits must be managed after the project ends.' }, L => {
  H(L, STORY('3.4', 'Part of something bigger', `
    ${SAY('bho', 'Saarthi is one of five projects in our Smart Mobility programme: smart cards, depot CCTV, electric-bus charging, a new fare system, and your Saarthi. Our programme director, Mrs Kale, coordinates all five.')}
    ${SAY('riya', 'Why not just run five separate projects?')}
    ${SAY('bho', 'Because the smart card must work in your app, the fare system feeds your ticketing, and all five compete for the same IT staff at PCT. Managed together, they give benefits none gives alone.')}
    ${SAY('kul', 'And notice: Saarthi will end the day PCT accepts it. But the benefits, more riders, less cash leakage, only show up months later. Someone has to manage those benefits after the project team has gone home.')}`));
  H(L, `<h2>1 · What is a programme?</h2>
    ${IDEA('Definition (D. C. Ferns)', '"A group of projects that are managed in a co-ordinated way to gain benefits that would not be possible were the projects to be managed independently."')}
    <h3>Five types of programme</h3>
    ${TBL(['Type', 'Projects that …', 'Smart Mobility example'], [['<b>Strategic</b>', 'implement a single strategy', 'All five projects implement PCT\'s "cashless, trackable city bus" strategy'], ['<b>Business cycle</b>', 'an organisation undertakes within a planning cycle', 'PCT\'s IT projects approved in this year\'s budget'], ['<b>Infrastructure</b>', 'identify common infrastructure, implement and maintain it', 'A shared data centre and network for all depots'], ['<b>Research & development</b>', 'develop new innovative products based on research', 'Predicting arrival times with machine learning'], ['<b>Innovative partnerships</b>', 'are based on collaboration between different organisations', 'PCT + a bank + a telecom company for the smart card']])}
    <h3>Programme manager vs project manager</h3>`);
  H(L, SVG(640, 240, `${bx(10, 10, 300, 34, 'Programme manager (Mrs Kale)', { c: 'pur' })}${bx(330, 10, 300, 34, 'Project manager (Riya)', { c: 'blu' })}
    ${[['many simultaneous projects', 'one project at a time'], ['impersonal relationship with resources', 'personal relationship with skilled resources'], ['minimise demand for resources', 'optimise use of resources'], ['projects tend to be seen as similar', 'projects tend to be seen as unique']].map(([a, b2], i) => `<g${S(0)}>${bx(10, 54 + i * 46, 300, 38, a, { c: 's2' })}${bx(330, 54 + i * 46, 300, 38, b2, { c: 's2' })}</g>`).join('')}`));
  H(L, `<h2>2 · Strategic programmes (the OGC approach)</h2>
    <p>A strategic programme is a portfolio of projects that all contribute to a common objective, managed with the UK Office of Government Commerce (OGC) approach. Its documents, in order:</p>`);
  stepper(L, 'From mandate to blueprint', SVG(660, 180, `
    ${[['Programme\nmandate', 'pur'], ['Programme\nbrief', 'blu'], ['Vision\nstatement', 'amb'], ['Blueprint', 'grn']].map(([t, c], i) => `<g${S(i)}>${bx(10 + i * 165, 50, 140, 60, t, { c })}${i < 3 ? ar(150 + i * 165, 80, 173 + i * 165, 80) : ''}</g>`).join('')}
    ${tx(80, 140, 'new services, how the\norganisation improves,\nfit with its goals', { c: 'xs mu', at: S(0) })}${tx(245, 140, '≈ feasibility study;\ncosts and benefits', { c: 'xs mu', at: S(1) })}${tx(410, 140, 'the new capability\nthe organisation will have', { c: 'xs mu', at: S(2) })}${tx(575, 140, 'changes needed to\nobtain the capability', { c: 'xs mu', at: S(3) })}
    ${tx(80, 24, 'programme director appointed\nas champion', { c: 'xs pur', at: S(0) })}`),
    ['The initial planning document is the <b>programme mandate</b>: the new services/capabilities the programme should deliver, how the organisation will be improved, and how it fits existing organisational goals. A <b>programme director</b> is appointed as champion for the scheme.', 'The <b>programme brief</b>: the equivalent of a feasibility study, with emphasis on costs and benefits.', 'The <b>vision statement</b>: explains the new capability the organisation will have.', 'The <b>blueprint</b>: explains the changes to be made to obtain the new capability.']);
  H(L, `<p><b>Aids to programme management</b>: projects in a programme may have physical and technical <b>dependencies</b> (the app needs the smart-card interface; ticketing needs the new fare system). These are drawn as <b>dependency diagrams</b>, similar to activity networks (week 6).</p>`);
  H(L, SVG(640, 170, `${bx(10, 20, 150, 44, 'New fare system', { c: 'blu' })}${bx(10, 100, 150, 44, 'Smart cards', { c: 'blu' })}${bx(250, 60, 150, 44, 'Saarthi ticketing', { c: 'amb' })}${bx(490, 20, 140, 44, 'Depot CCTV', { c: 'grn' })}${bx(490, 100, 140, 44, 'E-bus charging', { c: 'grn' })}
    ${ar(160, 42, 248, 76)}${ar(160, 122, 248, 90)}${tx(560, 160, 'independent: can run in parallel', { c: 'xs mu' })}`));
  H(L, `<h2>3 · Benefits management</h2>
    <p>Providing an organisation with a capability does <b>not</b> guarantee the envisaged benefits. Developers build the application, users use it, and only then does the organisation benefit. Because this happens <b>after the project has been completed</b>, benefits management is done at <b>programme level</b>.</p>`);
  H(L, SVG(640, 130, `${bx(10, 40, 150, 50, 'Developers', { c: 'blu' })}${ar(160, 65, 232, 65)}${tx(196, 50, 'build', { c: 'xs mu' })}${bx(234, 40, 150, 50, 'Users', { c: 'amb' })}${ar(384, 65, 456, 65)}${tx(420, 50, 'use', { c: 'xs mu' })}${bx(458, 40, 170, 50, 'Organisation', { c: 'grn' })}${tx(543, 110, 'benefits (after the project)', { c: 'xs grn' })}${tx(310, 112, '↑ the project ends at hand-over', { c: 'xs red' })}`));
  H(L, `${STEPS(['<b>Define</b> the expected benefits', '<b>Analyse</b> the balance between costs and benefits', '<b>Plan</b> how the benefits will be achieved', '<b>Allocate responsibilities</b> for their achievement', '<b>Monitor</b> achievement of the benefits'])}
    <h3>Kinds of benefit</h3>
    <div class="flowline">${['Mandatory requirement', 'Improved quality of service', 'Increased productivity', 'More motivated workforce', 'Internal management benefits', 'Risk reduction', 'Economies', 'Revenue enhancement / acceleration', 'Strategic fit'].map(t => `<span class="st">${t}</span>`).join('')}</div>
    <h3>Quantifying benefits</h3>
    ${TBL(['Benefit can be …', 'Lecture example', 'Saarthi example'], [['<b>Quantified and valued</b>', 'a reduction of x staff saving £y', 'cash leakage down Rs. 2 crore a year'], ['<b>Quantified but not valued</b>', 'a decrease in customer complaints by x %', 'complaints about late buses down 40 %'], ['<b>Identified but not easily quantified</b>', 'public approval for an organisation in its locality', 'Punekars trust city buses more']])}`);
  choice(L, 'Who is best placed to make sure Saarthi actually increases ridership a year after go-live?', [
    ['Riya, the project manager', 'Her project is closed by then and the team reassigned; benefits come after the project.', false],
    ['The programme (Mrs Kale), through benefits management', 'Right: benefits management is done at programme level because benefits arrive after projects end.', true],
    ['The GPS vendor', 'The vendor supplies hardware; it does not own PCT\'s business benefits.', false]]);
  quickcheck(L, [['Ferns\' definition of a programme?', 'A group of projects managed in a co-ordinated way to gain benefits not possible if managed independently.'], ['Name the five types of programme.', 'Strategic, business cycle, infrastructure, research & development, innovative partnerships.'], ['Order the OGC strategic programme documents.', 'Programme mandate → programme brief → vision statement → blueprint.']]);
  H(L, RECAP('Programme (Ferns) = coordinated group of projects for benefits not possible independently.', 'Types: strategic, business cycle, infrastructure, R&D, innovative partnerships.', 'Programme manager: many projects, impersonal, minimise resource demand, projects similar. Project manager: one project, personal, optimise resource use, project unique.', 'OGC strategic programme: mandate (+ programme director as champion) → brief → vision statement → blueprint; dependency diagrams.', 'Benefits management at programme level: define, analyse, plan, allocate responsibility, monitor. Benefits quantified & valued / quantified not valued / identified not quantified.'));
  H(L, NEXT('Saarthi is approved, funded and placed in its programme. Now the hardest question in the whole course: how big is it, and how long will it take?', 't-estbasics', 'Chapter 4.1 · How big is Saarthi?'));
});
